## Why This Is Asked

The parity check is a common error-detection primitive in data blocks and crypto pipelines. This task tests ability to perform a full 128-bit reduction with pure bitwise ops, no branches, and constant time.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value represented as hi/lo.
- Parity reduction via XOR folding.
- Branchless implementation for performance.

## Code Example

```javascript
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (int)(x & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt parity128 for native 128-bit ints on platforms with __int128?
- What are the trade-offs if you can't rely on unsigned shifts across all compilers?

## Why This Is Asked

In high-throughput diffing pipelines, diffusion-boosting byte-wise bit reversal can be done with a fixed, branchless sequence, testing mastery of multi-stage bit manipulations.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit-twiddling across 8-byte lanes
- Masks: 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F, 0x3333333333333333, 0x5555555555555555
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverseBitsInBytes64(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 4n) | (n << 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  n = ((n >>> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >>> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  return n;
}
```

