## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of 64-bit bit reversals and cross-half manipulation without loops. It ensures ability to compose operations on 128-bit types.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit bit reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct handling
- Non-branching composition

## Code Example
```javascript
// Outline only: reverse64 implemented with a sequence of mask/shifts
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1 | (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2 | (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) << 4 | (x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = ((x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x << 48) | ((x & 0xffff) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0xffff);
  return x;
}
```

